          "settings": {
            "heading": "We read the science. We don't run it.",
            "body": "<p>Biotaderm reviews published research and formulates on the basis of what it shows. We have not run our own clinical trials, and our copy never implies we have.</p><p>So the copy credits the researchers. When the research supports something, we say the research supports it, and we never present a finding as our own.</p>",
            "variant": "plain",
            "set_piece": "none",
            "ethos_icon": "none"
          }
        },
        "b_wording": {
          "type": "band",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why the wording matters",
            "body": "<p>You will notice the same phrases throughout the site. The research suggests. Studies indicate. The evidence points to. Those openings put the credit where it belongs.</p><p>The moment a sentence hands Biotaderm the discovery, with an \"our research shows\" or a \"we've proven\", it stops being true. Here is the test we apply to our own copy. \"Our research shows rosehip oil repairs the skin\" becomes \"the research on cold-pressed rosehip oil points to its carotenoids and fatty acids.\" Same ingredient, same story, told in a way we can stand behind.</p>",
            "variant": "plain",
            "set_piece": "chips",
            "ethos_icon": "none",
            "chips": "The research suggests|Studies indicate|The evidence points to"
